  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Integrating TB with MCH services in Malawi</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tuberculosis (TB) is treatable, but is a leading cause of death in Malawi because less than half of all TB cases are detected. Pregnant women with TB are twice as likely to give birth to a premature or a small baby and four times more likely to die during childbirth than healthy women. This project will teach people to recognise the symptoms of TB in women and children, and to get treatment; it will also teach them to prevent the disease. Health workers will be trained to screen women for TB during antenatal visits and to screen children when they attend for routine care. Those with signs of TB will be helped to travel to health centres for diagnostic tests and treatment, which are free from government health facilities. Volunteers will help people adhere to their medicine. TB screening is not part of maternal and child health services in Malawi. Since these services are well-used, they provide a good opportunity to identity women and children with TB. The project will document the process and show how addressing TB and maternal and child health together can improve the health of women and children.</narrative>
  </description>
